The '''Zong national football team''' represents [[Zong]] in men's international association football. The team is governed by the [[Zong Football Association]] and is one of the traditional powers of the [[AFA World Cup]], having won the inaugural [[1958 AFA World Cup|1958 tournament]]. Zong are currently 17th in the [[AFA Men's World Ranking]] and opened their [[2026 AFA World Cup|2026 World Cup]] campaign with a 1–0 victory over [[Balisca national football team|Balisca]].

== History ==

=== Early success ===
Zong's place in AFA football was established immediately at the start of the tournament era, when the national team won the inaugural 1958 AFA World Cup. That triumph remains the defining achievement in the team's history and made Zong one of the first nations to set the standard in continental football.

In the decades that followed, Zong remained a respected side in East Fosia, with a reputation for technical discipline and tactical organisation. While the team has not added a second World Cup title, it has continued to produce competitive squads and remains one of the better-known national sides in the region.

=== Modern era ===
In the modern era, Zong have stayed within the upper half of the AFA ranking tables and continue to be considered a difficult opponent for any regional side. Their 2026 World Cup campaign began strongly with a 1–0 win over Balisca on opening day, a result that underlined the team's ability to compete with higher-ranked opponents on the biggest stage.

== Stadium ==

Zong play their home matches at [[Huwei National Stadium]] in [[Huwei]], the capital and largest city of the country. The stadium is the main venue for the national team and the symbolic centre of Zong football.

== Playing style ==

Zong are generally described as a technically polished and tactically disciplined team. Their football is typically built around compact defending, controlled possession, and efficient use of midfield passing lanes, with an emphasis on maintaining shape over all-out attacking play.

